Subject: page map: enter every slice a page's block area spans
Fixes: S1, A2 - huge pages were entered in the page map only up to ~4 MiB, and every page's slice count came from its size, not its span, so mi_is_in_heap_region missed live addresses (and over-entered one slice past OS-aligned pages)
Upstream: to report (the clamp); the exact count is upstream 221e58feb, not yet released

mi_page_map_get_idx (src/heap/page-map.c) decides which 64 KiB slices
_mi_page_map_register and _mi_page_map_unregister enter for a page.
v3.6.1 had two faults there:

1. It cut page_size to MI_LARGE_PAGE_SIZE - MI_ARENA_SLICE_SIZE ("furthest
   interior pointer"), so only about the first 4 MiB of a huge page was
   entered. mi_is_in_heap_region, mi_any_heap_contains and _mi_safe_ptr_page
   returned "not ours" for any address deeper into a live huge block. STXlib's
   Debug builds therefore refused to release their own blocks aligned at
   4 MiB or more (audit S1). The clamp is removed: the whole block area is
   entered. Cost: one entry per slice, about 16,000 per GiB, written on a
   huge page's allocation and again on its free; and the sub-maps it needs
   (64 KiB each, covering 512 MiB of address range) are committed on demand
   and kept until exit, as all sub-maps are. That is about 128 KiB per GiB
   of address range that huge blocks have used, 1/8192 of it (review
   F-0003-1; a successful 1 TiB block commits about 128 MiB of page map).
   The alternative, a clamp at 256 MiB + 64 KiB (mimalloc refuses
   alignments of 256 MiB or more), would cover every address STXlib's
   tagged release asks about, but would leave mi_is_in_heap_region wrong
   for deeper addresses; the patch keeps it right for all of them.
   mi_page_map_set_range_prim already walks across sub-maps, and its
   failure path resets what it set (set_range, unchanged).
2. It counted slices as _mi_slice_count_of_size(page_size) plus
   (page_start - slice_start) / slice size. That under-counts when the area
   starts inside a slice and crosses one more boundary: the tail of the last
   block of many large pages went unentered (audit A2). It over-counts for
   OS-aligned pages, whose area starts a whole number of slices after the
   slice start: one slice past the page's end was entered, and unregistering
   could clear a neighbour's entry. Upstream fixed this in 221e58feb
   (2026-11-08) by counting the slices that [page_start, page_start +
   page_size) spans. The patch takes that line unchanged.

Registration and unregistration both use this function, so they stay
symmetric. mi_free's fast path finds page metadata by alignment
(MI_PAGE_META_IS_ALIGNED) and does not read these entries. The checked
lookups (_mi_safe_ptr_page and its callers in heap.c and arena.c) now find
the page for every address inside a block.

--- a/src/heap/page-map.c
+++ b/src/heap/page-map.c
@@ -273,8 +273,12 @@
 static size_t mi_page_map_get_idx(mi_page_t* page, size_t* sub_idx, size_t* slice_count) {
   size_t page_size;
   uint8_t* page_start = mi_page_area(page, &page_size);
-  if (page_size > MI_LARGE_PAGE_SIZE) { page_size = MI_LARGE_PAGE_SIZE - MI_ARENA_SLICE_SIZE; }  // furthest interior pointer
-  *slice_count = _mi_slice_count_of_size(page_size) + ((page_start - mi_page_slice_start(page))/MI_ARENA_SLICE_SIZE); // add for large aligned blocks
+  // STXlib: no clamp to MI_LARGE_PAGE_SIZE ("furthest interior pointer"), so every address of a huge
+  // block is found and mi_is_in_heap_region answers for all of it (STXlib audit S1).
+  mi_assert_internal(page_size > 0);
+  // count the slices spanned by `[page_start, page_start + page_size)` exactly (upstream 221e58feb)
+  const mi_addr_t start = mi_addr(page_start);
+  *slice_count = ((start + page_size - 1) / MI_ARENA_SLICE_SIZE) - (start / MI_ARENA_SLICE_SIZE) + 1;
   return mi_page_map_index(page_start, sub_idx);
 }
 
